      "In South Korea, Cheondogyo retained greater autonomy and remained closer to its traditional doctrines and practices, though it suffered from internal factional conflicts, particularly over leadership succession, the interpretation of innaecheon doctrine, and the religion's relationship to politics and modernity. Various Cheondogyo organizations and temples have operated semi-independently, with loose coordination through the Cheondogyo Central Headquarters in Seoul. The religion's declining membership and social influence have reduced internal diversity, though theological debates continue among Cheondogyo scholars and clergy about how to interpret the tradition in contemporary contexts and how to revitalize the movement."
